{"term": "frigid", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "insults", "subcategory": "low_desire_female", "concept": "sexual_frigidity", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "dated_slang", "crude": 38, "technical": 12, "recognition_pct_current": 72, "recognition_pct_peak": 85, "peak_year": 1965, "regional": null, "definition": "Lacking sexual responsiveness or desire; sexually cold and unresponsive (most commonly applied pejoratively to women).", "english_term": "frigid", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Strongly gendered: almost always directed at women who decline sex, reflecting mid-20th-century expectation of female sexual availability; clinical psychology replaced it with 'hypoactive sexual desire disorder'; still widely recognized but considered sexist."}
{"term": "asexual", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "identities_orientations", "subcategory": "asexuality_identity", "concept": "asexuality", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "clinical", "crude": 0, "technical": 65, "recognition_pct_current": 82, "recognition_pct_peak": 82, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Experiencing little or no sexual attraction to others; used both as a clinical descriptor and as a sexual identity label.", "english_term": "asexual", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Also a biological term for asexual reproduction; the identity usage was popularized by AVEN (Asexuality Visibility and Education Network) in the 2000s and is now mainstream in LGBTQ+ discourse."}
{"term": "low libido", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "low_desire_clinical", "concept": "low_libido", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "clinical", "crude": 5, "technical": 75, "recognition_pct_current": 82, "recognition_pct_peak": 82, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A reduced or diminished level of sexual drive or desire.", "english_term": "low libido", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "'Libido' is Freudian terminology absorbed into common medical usage; the compound phrase appears frequently in pharmaceutical advertising for testosterone replacement therapy and hormonal treatments; applies to any gender."}
{"term": "repressed", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "sexual_repression", "concept": "sexual_repression", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 10, "technical": 30, "recognition_pct_current": 85, "recognition_pct_peak": 85, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Suppressing or holding back sexual feelings, desires, or identity, typically due to cultural, religious, or psychological forces.", "english_term": "repressed", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Psychoanalytic origin (Freud's repression mechanism); widely absorbed into lay speech; often implies the suppressed desire will eventually emerge problematically; 'sexually repressed' is the full common phrase."}
{"term": "prudish", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "sexual_modesty", "concept": "prudishness", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 15, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 78, "recognition_pct_peak": 78, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Excessively modest or easily shocked by sexual content; overly fastidious about propriety in sexual matters.", "english_term": "prudish", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "From 'prude' (17th-century French); mildly pejorative suggesting oversensitivity; gender-neutral though historically more applied to women; gentler insult than 'frigid'."}
{"term": "uptight", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "sexual_inhibition", "concept": "sexual_inhibition", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 10, "technical": 0, "recognition_pct_current": 88, "recognition_pct_peak": 92, "peak_year": 1972, "regional": null, "definition": "Tense, anxious, and unable to relax, especially about sexuality; inhibited and overly conventional.", "english_term": "uptight", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "1960s–70s counterculture slang; sexual inhibition is one application of a broader meaning covering general rigidity and anxiety; still in common use but slightly dated in its specifically sexual connotation."}
{"term": "strait-laced", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "sexual_conservatism", "concept": "sexual_conservatism", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 5, "technical": 0, "recognition_pct_current": 60, "recognition_pct_peak": 72, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Having very strict moral standards, especially regarding sexuality and social propriety; rigidly conventional.", "english_term": "strait-laced", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Metaphor from tightly laced corsets constraining the body; also spelled 'straight-laced'; applies to moral conservatism generally, not sex exclusively; formal, older usage."}
{"term": "hypersexual", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "sexual_excess_clinical", "concept": "hypersexuality", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "clinical", "crude": 12, "technical": 82, "recognition_pct_current": 58, "recognition_pct_peak": 58, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Having an abnormally or compulsively high level of sexual urges, thoughts, or behavior (clinical adjective form).", "english_term": "hypersexual", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Adjective form; shares concept 'hypersexuality' with the noun form; DSM-5 proposed but did not include 'hypersexual disorder'; sometimes conflated with sex addiction in lay usage."}
{"term": "hypersexuality", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "sexual_excess_clinical", "concept": "hypersexuality", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "clinical", "crude": 10, "technical": 88, "recognition_pct_current": 52, "recognition_pct_peak": 52, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A clinical condition characterized by compulsive and excessive sexual fantasies, urges, or behaviors that cause distress or functional impairment.", "english_term": "hypersexuality", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Noun form; shares concept with 'hypersexual'; proposed as DSM-5 'hypersexual disorder' but excluded; commonly equated with 'sex addiction' in popular discourse despite lacking official diagnostic status."}
{"term": "nymphomaniac", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "insults", "subcategory": "female_hypersexuality", "concept": "nymphomania", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "dated_slang", "crude": 42, "technical": 38, "recognition_pct_current": 82, "recognition_pct_peak": 88, "peak_year": 1978, "regional": null, "definition": "A woman believed to have an insatiable or compulsive need for sex (originally a clinical diagnosis; now typically pejorative or humorous).", "english_term": "nymphomaniac", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "19th-century clinical diagnosis; gendered exclusively female (male equivalent 'satyriasis' is barely known); reflects historical double standard; clinically replaced by 'compulsive sexual behavior disorder'; shares concept 'nymphomania' with 'nympho'."}
{"term": "nympho", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "insults", "subcategory": "female_hypersexuality", "concept": "nymphomania", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 58, "technical": 8, "recognition_pct_current": 78, "recognition_pct_peak": 82, "peak_year": 1982, "regional": null, "definition": "Shortened form of nymphomaniac; a woman with an allegedly insatiable sexual appetite (crude and pejorative).", "english_term": "nympho", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "More casual and crude than the full form; shares concept 'nymphomania' with 'nymphomaniac'; occasionally reclaimed by some women but predominantly deployed as a derogatory label."}
